 ## Overview
-Shading models for volume rendering apply local illumination calculations to enhance the perception of 3D shape and surface orientation within volumetric data [S1, unofficial]. Unlike global illumination techniques that simulate light transport through participating media, this method approximates surface-like appearance by treating scalar field gradients as pseudo-normals and applying standard reflection models (diffuse and specular) to the volume samples [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from [Gradient-based Shading with Phase Functions](1020-gradient-based-shading-with-phase-functions.md) by using simplified local reflection rather than complex phase functions, and from [Frequency Domain Directional Shading](852-frequency-domain-directional-shading.md) by operating in the spatial domain [S1, unofficial].
+Shading models for volume rendering apply local illumination calculations to enhance the perception of 3D shape and surface orientation within volumetric data [S1, unofficial]. Unlike global illumination techniques that simulate light transport through participating media, this method approximates surface-like appearance by treating scalar field gradients as pseudo-normals and applying standard reflection models (diffuse and specular) to the volume samples [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from [Gradient-based Shading with Phase Functions](1020-gradient-based-shading-with-phase-functions.md) by using simplified local reflection rather than complex phase functions [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The method evaluates shading contributions along a ray traversing the volume. It determines whether a region of the ray should be shaded based on data values and gradient magnitudes at the entry and exit points of that region. If shading is applied, it computes diffuse and specular components using averaged data values and gradients from the region boundaries.
+The method evaluates shading contributions along a ray traversing the volume [S1, unofficial]. It determines whether a region of the ray should be shaded based on data values and gradient magnitudes at the entry and exit points of that region [S1, unofficial]. If shading is applied, it computes diffuse and specular components using averaged data values and gradients from the region boundaries [S1, unofficial].
 
