 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity**: $O(1)$ per shading evaluation, assuming gradient computation is $O(1)$ (e.g., via trilinear derivative). The cost is added to the ray casting loop.
-*   **Memory Complexity**: $O(1)$ additional memory for shading calculations.
+*   **Time Complexity**: $O(1)$ per shading evaluation, assuming gradient computation is $O(1)$ (e.g., via trilinear derivative). The cost is added to the ray casting loop [G].
+*   **Memory Complexity**: $O(1)$ additional memory for shading calculations [G].
 *   **Measured Performance**: Shading adds computational overhead to ray casting but is generally cheaper than global illumination techniques. It helps considerably in bringing out features and establishing orientations [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Scaling**: Performance scales linearly with the number of ray samples. Parallelization is effective as each ray's shading computation is independent [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths**: Enhances perception of 3D shape and surface detail; computationally efficient; easy to implement within existing ray casting frameworks.
-*   **Limitations**: Approximates surface-like appearance; does not account for subsurface scattering or global light transport; gradient discontinuities may cause artifacts; limited to local illumination.
-*   **When to Use**: When enhancing visual clarity of features in volumetric data is prioritized over physical accuracy of light transport.
-*   **When Not to Use**: When accurate simulation of participating media light transport (e.g., subsurface scattering, global illumination) is required.
